Idiopathic Hyperhidrosis
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Excessive uncontrollable bilateral sweating, Independent of temperature/exertion, Cessation during sleep, Normal systemic metabolic screening

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-025
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute maceration and suspected secondary infection of the palmar and axillary skin leading to severe pain and functional distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with severe, acute skin breakdown and severe localized pain in the axillae and palms secondary to intractable sweating, severely impacting the ability to perform basic hand functions. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively diagnose or exclude acute secondary bacterial cellulitis or fungal intertrigo, and to provide immediate targeted dermatological management prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hands and underarms are dripping sweat constantly even though it's freezing, and now the skin is completely raw, tearing, and burning."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 5-day history of acutely worsening skin maceration directly resulting from excessive, unpredictable, and uncontrollable bilateral sweating. The hyperhidrosis predominantly involves the palms, soles, and axillae, occurring entirely independent of ambient temperature or physical exertion. The patient notes complete cessation of the excessive sweating strictly during sleep. The constant dampness has led to severe social embarrassment and functional impairment (inability to grip a steering wheel). Patient denies severe night sweats, chronic fever, or significant unintentional weight loss. The raw skin has become intensely painful today.
Functional Status: Independent but avoiding using hands due to severe pain from macerated fissures.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound social isolation and acute occupational anxiety due to the visual and physical symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, extremely self-conscious, holding hands away from the body with visible droplets of sweat actively forming on the palms in a cool room.
Physical Exam: Bilateral palms and axillae exhibit profuse, active watery sweating. The axillary folds show extensive, severe maceration (white, water-logged skin) with superficial fissuring. Erythema is present, but there is a complete absence of deep induration, spreading cellulitic changes, fluctuance, or purulent foul-smelling discharge. Complete absence of generalized lymphadenopathy. Documented history of a normal comprehensive metabolic panel, normal thyroid function tests, and negative infectious screening confirming the absolute absence of a secondary systemic cause.
Environmental Observations: Ambient temperature in the room is cool (19°C); patient is wearing a thin t-shirt.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted over-the-counter clinical strength antiperspirants with intense burning and zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Idiopathic Hyperhidrosis - Status: Complicated by acute severe maceration
Differentials:

Secondary Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out by the complete absence of spreading warmth, deep induration, purulent discharge, or systemic fever.

Hyperthyroidism - Ruled out explicitly by the normal resting heart rate, complete cessation of sweating during sleep, and previously documented normal thyroid function tests.

Lymphoma/Malignancy (B-Symptoms) - Ruled out as the patient explicitly notes the sweating stops entirely during sleep (lack of night sweats) and has no unintentional weight loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to acute, severe pain and functional impairment resulting from extensive skin breakdown and maceration driven by uncontrolled hyperhidrosis. The localized pain and erythema strongly mimicked an acute, rapidly progressive soft tissue infection (cellulitis or severe intertrigo). An immediate clinical examination was mandatory to accurately differentiate between benign, severe moisture damage and an infectious process requiring urgent systemic antibiotics. By confirming the absence of deep infection and prescribing targeted, high-strength topical barrier and drying therapies, the physician safely prevented severe dermatological complications and an unnecessary emergency department visit, satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of topical 1% Hydrocortisone cream for the axillary fissures to rapidly reduce acute inflammation, and a potent topical antiperspirant (Aluminium Chloride Hexahydrate 20%) to be applied strictly at night.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply the strong antiperspirant strictly to completely dry skin right before going to sleep, rather than during the day. Because sweat glands are dormant during sleep, the medication can physically plug the ducts without being washed away, rewarding the patient with vastly decreased daytime sweating and preventing further agonizing skin tearing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a dermatologist for consideration of Botox injections or iontophoresis.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to assess skin healing and refer for definitive hyperhidrosis management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or GP review if developing spreading red streaks from the armpits, a high systemic fever, or if the areas begin leaking foul-smelling yellow pus.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes previously normal metabolic panels remain accurate; the development of severe sweating strictly at night while asleep mandates immediate re-investigation.
Clinical Course: Primary hyperhidrosis is a benign overactivity of the sympathetic nervous system. While the skin maceration is painful, the condition itself does not indicate an underlying disease.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if you notice red streaks spreading from your armpits, or if the skin starts producing thick, yellow, foul-smelling pus.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your sweat glands are essentially stuck in the 'on' position due to an overactive nerve signal. The severe pain is simply from your skin becoming completely waterlogged and tearing, exactly like spending too long in a bath. We must physically plug the glands overnight to allow the skin to dry and heal.
Actionable Steps: Use a hair dryer on a cool setting to completely dry your armpits before applying the nighttime medication. Applying aluminium chloride to wet skin creates a mild acid that causes intense burning; applying it to perfectly dry skin ensures it works painlessly and effectively, reinforcing adherence to the treatment.

